# Force LF line endings on files where CRLF would break runtime behaviour.
#
# Why this file exists: on Windows clones, git defaults to converting LF
# to CRLF on checkout. When the agent image Dockerfile COPYs these
# scripts into the container, the CRLFs are preserved. The kernel then
# parses `#!/bin/bash\r\n` and looks for an interpreter literally named
# `/bin/bash\r` (with a carriage return), which doesn't exist. The
# container exits 255 with the confusing error
# `exec /usr/local/bin/harness-cli: no such file or directory` — pointing
# at the script even though the script itself is fine. This file
# prevents that on future clones; the agent Dockerfile's `sed -i 's/\r$//'`
# step is the defensive belt-and-braces for clones that already happened.

# Harness scripts run inside Linux containers — must be LF regardless of
# host OS.
harness/*.sh    text eol=lf
harness/*.ts    text eol=lf

# Shell scripts in general.
*.sh            text eol=lf

# Dockerfiles are read by the daemon (Linux) and should also be LF.
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile    text eol=lf
